WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.920
It's May 13th, 2026. Here's what changed overnight in the environmental regulatory landscape.

00:00:08.020 --> 00:00:13.860
Alberta Pipeline Project now contingent on federal provincial agreement on industrial carbon

00:00:13.860 --> 00:00:21.600
tax. Alberta and federal officials have tied a new west coast pipeline to resolution of industrial

00:00:21.600 --> 00:00:28.820
carbon pricing. British Columbia has advanced six clean electricity projects to cut emissions at

00:00:28.820 --> 00:00:35.420
industrial sites. Environment and climate change Canada is directing research funding toward

00:00:35.420 --> 00:00:42.080
plastic pollution effects on indigenous communities. Alberta and Canada have conditioned approval

00:00:42.080 --> 00:00:47.520
of a new pipeline to the west coast on reaching agreement on the industrial carbon tax.

00:00:48.340 --> 00:00:54.060
The linkage directly affects project economics for any proponent advancing linear infrastructure

00:00:54.060 --> 00:01:02.840
under the Impact Assessment Act and Alberta EPA. Prior pipeline applications treated carbon

00:01:02.840 --> 00:01:09.440
pricing as a separate compliance stream. The current arrangement folds tax outcomes into the overall

00:01:09.440 --> 00:01:16.120
permitting decision. Consultants preparing environmental impact statements for oil and gas corridors

00:01:16.780 --> 00:01:22.980
must now model multiple carbon price scenarios in their net present value and risk assessments.

00:01:24.060 --> 00:01:30.100
Field teams should update greenhouse gas quantification protocols to align with the latest federal

00:01:30.100 --> 00:01:37.220
draft guidance on industrial emissions. Watch for the next federal provincial meeting outcome which

00:01:37.220 --> 00:01:43.260
will determine whether the project proceeds to detailed regulatory review or requires redesign.

00:01:44.540 --> 00:01:51.820
In British Columbia, the government has approved funding for six made in BC clean electricity projects.

00:01:52.540 --> 00:01:59.120
These projects target facilities already subject to provincial greenhouse gas reporting and

00:01:59.120 --> 00:02:03.440
potential future performance standards under the Environmental Management Act.

00:02:04.260 --> 00:02:09.960
Proponents of new or expanding operations should review eligibility criteria this week

00:02:09.960 --> 00:02:15.640
to determine whether on-site electrification can offset anticipated carbon costs.

00:02:16.280 --> 00:02:23.200
The federal government is funding six research projects examining plastic pollution impacts on

00:02:23.200 --> 00:02:29.860
indigenous communities. Results are expected to inform future CPA risk assessments and potential

00:02:29.860 --> 00:02:36.980
additions to schedule one toxic substances. Practitioners conducting site investigations near northern

00:02:36.980 --> 00:02:43.720
or coastal communities should incorporate microplastic sampling into baseline programs or sediment

00:02:43.720 --> 00:02:51.300
or surface water pathways exist. The Alberta Canada Pipeline Agreement introduces new uncertainty

00:02:51.300 --> 00:02:57.340
into project schedules and capital planning for any proponent relying on federal or provincial

00:02:57.340 --> 00:03:04.560
approvals. Environmental consultants should prepare sensitivity analyses that treat carbon tax levels

00:03:04.560 --> 00:03:11.020
as a variable input rather than a fixed compliance cost. Early engagement with both Alberta

00:03:11.020 --> 00:03:18.280
environment and protected areas and the impact assessment a GIC is now advisable for any linear

00:03:18.280 --> 00:03:24.320
project crossing provincial boundaries. If you are working on pipeline or linear infrastructure

00:03:24.320 --> 00:03:31.020
environmental impact statements in Alberta, review the latest federal draft industrial carbon pricing

00:03:31.020 --> 00:03:38.700
guidance for direct implications. Update internal remedial options analysis templates to include multiple

00:03:38.700 --> 00:03:45.560
carbon price scenarios when modeling thermal or energy intensive technologies in western Canada.

00:03:46.240 --> 00:03:52.160
Brief clients on the BC clean electricity funding program for any industrial site considering

00:03:52.160 --> 00:04:00.060
on-site generation or grid upgrades to reduce emissions liabilities. Incorporate microplastic

00:04:00.060 --> 00:04:06.620
sampling protocols into baseline surface water programs for sites near indigenous communities where

00:04:06.620 --> 00:04:15.420
ECCC funded research may influence future CPA listings. Monitor federal provincial discussions on

00:04:15.420 --> 00:04:22.240
industrial carbon tax for pipeline projects. Any agreement could trigger immediate impact assessment

00:04:22.240 --> 00:04:30.100
agency process updates. Track ECCC release of initial findings from the six plastic pollution

00:04:30.100 --> 00:04:38.500
research projects for potential additions to CPA schedule one. Review BC clean tech funding intake

00:04:38.500 --> 00:04:44.500
deadlines for electrification projects that may qualify under current provincial emissions performance

00:04:44.500 --> 00:04:52.520
standards. Now speaking of carbon price sensitivity in remediation cost modeling consider a former

00:04:52.520 --> 00:04:59.940
upstream oil and gas battery in Alberta where the phase two ESA has delineated a 1.2 hectare

00:04:59.940 --> 00:05:09.080
plume of PHC F2 and F3 in fine grain till. The client's internal model assumes a fixed $50

00:05:09.080 --> 00:05:15.500
per ton carbon price for the thermal disorption option. Yet the federal provincial pipeline

00:05:15.500 --> 00:05:20.640
talks signal that industrial rates could rise sharply within the project timeline.

00:05:21.280 --> 00:05:27.500
In the field this means the remedial options analysis must now run parallel cost curves

00:05:27.500 --> 00:05:36.800
at $50, $80, and $120 per ton. Each increment changes the break even point between excavation and

00:05:36.800 --> 00:05:44.720
in situ chemical oxidation. The experienced practitioner notices that carbon pricing also indirectly

00:05:44.720 --> 00:05:52.760
affects disposal facility gate fees and transportation surcharges. These items are rarely flagged in

00:05:52.760 --> 00:06:00.940
standard CCME tier two worksheets. The insight that separates senior from junior work is recognizing that

00:06:00.940 --> 00:06:08.200
the carbon tax applies not only to on-site energy use but to the embodied emissions of imported

00:06:08.200 --> 00:06:15.800
reagents and off-site treatment. The mistake I see most often is locking the remedial design to a

00:06:15.800 --> 00:06:22.260
single carbon price assumption in the initial options analysis. The fix is to require the cost

00:06:22.260 --> 00:06:29.100
model to carry three discrete carbon price scenarios through to the final recommendation table submitted

00:06:29.100 --> 00:06:38.900
under EPA. Tomorrow watch for any federal provincial agreement on industrial carbon tax that could

00:06:38.900 --> 00:06:44.280
immediately alter impact assessment agency timelines for pending pipeline projects.

00:06:45.100 --> 00:06:51.080
That covers today's environmental intelligence. If you found this useful, share it with a colleague

00:06:51.080 --> 00:06:57.780
who needs to stay current. We're back tomorrow morning. This episode used AI voice synthesis of

00:06:57.780 --> 00:07:02.200
my voice. Editorial selection and analysis are my own.
